protons). The calculation is based on HIJING predictions [42] for transverse momenta \([0.4,0.8]\) GeV/c (as the highest proper proton and anti-proton identification probabilities are estimated in this interval). In the terms of a pair:

\[Pair\;pur(k^{*})=Part\;pur_{1}(p_{T1})*Part\;pur_{2}(p_{T2}) \tag{6.3}\]

where \(Pair\;pur(k^{*})\) is a pair purity product of multiplying separate probabilities for single particles \(part_{1}\) and \(part_{2}\). \(Pair\;pur(k^{*})\) is a histogram which depends on many combinations of \(p_{T1}\) and \(p_{T2}\). In the terms of a pair, the probability that a pair is composed of primordial particles is \(0.25\) (not taking into account the track misidentification). Corrected correlation functions are calculated according to the formula:

\[C_{corr}(k^{*})=\frac{C_{meas}(k^{*})-1}{Pair\;pur(k^{*})}+1 \tag{6.4}\]

#### Resolution smearing correction

Momentum resolution is parametrized in the following way:

\[\frac{\triangle p_{i}}{p_{i}}=a_{p,i}+b_{p,i}p_{i}^{\alpha_{i}}+c_{p,i}p_{i} \tag{6.5}\]

\[\triangle\phi_{i}=a_{\phi,i}+b_{\phi,i}\phi_{i}^{\alpha_{i}} \tag{6.6}\]

\[\triangle\theta=a_{\theta,i}+b_{\theta,i}p_{i}^{\alpha_{i}} \tag{6.7}\]

where \(i\) denotes a particle type. For each particle, basing on its momentum \(p\) and angles \(\phi\) and \(\theta\) (the azimuthal angle with respect to the horizontal plane and the polar angle between the track and beam axis, respectively), corresponding smeared sigmas \(\sigma\bigtriangleup p\), \(\sigma\bigtriangleup\phi\) and \(\sigma\bigtriangleup\theta\) are found. Then, momentum resolution smears \(\partial p\), \(\partial\phi\) and \(\partial\theta\) are generated, from Gaussian distributions with zero mean values and corresponding sigmas; new, smeared momenta are calculated:

\[p_{x}^{smear}=p_{x}^{meas}+\partial p_{x} \tag{6.8}\]

\[p_{y}^{smear}=p_{y}^{meas}+\partial p_{y} \tag{6.9}\]

\[p_{z}^{smear}=p_{z}^{meas}+\partial p_{z} \tag{6.10}\]where \(\partial p_{x}\), \(\partial p_{y}\) and \(\partial p_{z}\) are the functions dependent on angles \(\phi\) and \(\theta\) and, respectively \(p_{x}\), \(p_{y}\) and \(p_{z}\).

#### Residual correlation correction

This correction allows one to estimate the impact of contamination originating from hyperon decays to the experimental \(p-p\), \(\overline{p}-\overline{p}\), \(p-\overline{p}\) correlation functions. A detailed description of the technique is presented in the following section.

