In such case when proton is faster (correlation function for \(k_{out}^{*}>0\)) it catches up to the slower anti-proton, so they stay close to each other longer, their effective interaction time is longer, hence the correlation effect is stronger. When proton is slower (correlation function for \(k_{out}^{*}<0\)), the faster anti-proton flies away fast, the time they spend close to each other is short, hence the effective interaction time is short and the correlation effect is smaller.

