## Appendix 2- Symbols and conventions used

This part define the formulas which are used for two-particle separation distributions. In terms of two-particle correlations, distinguishable particles \(1\) and \(2\) are considered. Each particle is described by its coordinates of freeze-out: spatial \(x\), \(y\), \(z\) and time: \(t\) (four-vector \(\textbf{x}=\{t,\overrightarrow{x}\}\)), and by its momentum coordinates: \(p_{x}\), \(p_{y}\), \(p_{z}\) (momentum) and \(E\) (energy) (four-momentum \(\textbf{p}=\{E,\overrightarrow{p}\}\)) in the reference frame of the emitting source (CMS). When two particles are combined into a pair, a new reference frame can be introduced, a frame where the center-of-mass of the pair rests. This frame is called Pair Rest Frame (PRF). Values in PRF are marked with asterisks *.

The total momentum of pair

\[\textbf{P}=\textbf{p}_{1}+\textbf{p}_{2}=2\textbf{p} \tag{9.1}\]

and the relative momentum of pair

\[\textbf{q}=\textbf{p}_{1}-\textbf{p}_{2} \tag{9.2}\]

are the most important quantities. For non-identical particles are generalized momentum difference is defined:

\[\tilde{q}=\textbf{q}-\textbf{P}(\textbf{q}\textbf{P})/\textbf{P}^{2} \tag{9.3}\]

In PRF the one has

\[\tilde{q}=\{0,2\overrightarrow{k}^{2}\} \tag{9.4}\]

where \(k^{*}\) is the momentum of first particle of pair in PRF. In CMS one also has the spatial coordinate

\[\textbf{X}=[(\textbf{P}_{1}\textbf{P})\textbf{x}_{1}+(\textbf{p}_{2}\textbf{ P})\textbf{x}_{2}]/\textbf{P}^{2} \tag{9.5}\]

Also new directions can be defined, similar to the \(out\), \(side\) and \(long\) decompositions of Bertsch and Pratt. The \(long\) direction is the direction of beam axis, or \(z\). In the transverse plane the \(out\) (or \(x\)) direction is the one of the momentum of the pair \(\overrightarrow{P}\), and \(side\) (or \(y\)) is perpendicular to \(out\) and \(long\).

Pair separation can also be calculated:

\[\overrightarrow{r}=\overrightarrow{r}_{1}^{*}-\overrightarrow{r}_{2} \tag{9.6}\]

The \(time\) difference:

\[\Delta t=t_{1}-t_{2} \tag{9.7}\]

Both of the variables can be calculated in the PRF and then they are denoted as \(\overrightarrow{r^{*}}\) and \(t^{*}\).

For non-identical particles it is significant which particle is first and which is second, as the signs of \(\overrightarrow{r}\) and \(\overrightarrow{q}\) depend on ordering. Therefore a convention is adopted that the first particle is always the one with lower mass, if both particles have the same masses, the one with larger value of charge is taken as first.

The procedure of decomposition of \(\overrightarrow{r^{*}}\) and \(\overrightarrow{k^{*}}\) into \(out\), \(side\) and \(long\) is described below. First, the pair is boosted to the Longitudinally Co-Moving System (LCMS), that is the system where:

\[p_{1,z}=-p_{2,z} \tag{9.8}\]

along the \(z\) axis with velocity

\[\beta_{z}=|p_{z}|/E \tag{9.9}\]

Then

\[k^{*}_{long}=p^{LCMS}_{1,z}=-p^{LCMS}_{2,z} \tag{9.10}\]

\[r^{*}_{long}=r_{1,z}-r_{2,z} \tag{9.11}\]

Then a pair is rotated so that its \(x\) axis is along the pair transverse momentum. Then a pair is boosted to the PRF along \(x\) axis with

\[\beta_{t}=|p_{t}|/(E^{2}-p_{z}^{2}) \tag{9.12}\]

Other components are given:

\[k^{*}_{out}=p^{PRF}_{1,x}=-p^{PRF}_{2,x} \tag{9.13}\]

and

\[k^{*}_{side}=p^{PRF}_{1,y}=-p^{PRF}_{2,y} \tag{9.14}\]

Similarly:

\[r^{*}_{out}=r^{PRF}_{1,x}-r^{PRF}_{2,x} \tag{9.15}\]\[r^{*}_{side}=r^{PRF}_{1,y}-r^{PRF}_{2,y} \tag{9.16}\]

The initial separation in CMS \(\overrightarrow{r}\) can also be decomposed into three components: \(r_{out}\), \(r_{side}\), \(r_{long}\). Simple relations between the pair separation in two reference frames are given:

\[r^{*}_{side}=r_{side} \tag{9.17}\]

\[r^{*}_{long}=\gamma_{z}(r_{long}-\beta_{z}\Delta t) \tag{9.18}\]

\[\Delta t_{LCMS}=\gamma_{z}(\Delta t-\beta_{z}r_{long}) \tag{9.19}\]

\[r^{*}_{out}=\gamma_{t}(r_{out}-\beta_{t}\Delta t_{LCMS}) \tag{9.20}\]

\[t^{*}=\gamma_{t}(\Delta t_{LCMS}-\beta_{t}r_{out}) \tag{9.21}\]

where \(\gamma_{z}=(1-\beta_{z}^{2})^{-1/2}\) and \(\gamma_{t}=(1-\beta_{t}^{2})^{-1/2}\).

